timestamp=2026-11-04T09:44:21.880Z level=INFO run=92c8de62 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Another look: the task says \"Disposal write-offs are rare and, when they occur, leave an unmistakable trace in the data.\"\n# Maybe some dispatches are \"written off\" items sent to disposal - possibly recorded with zero price? No prices in dispatches.\n# Or maybe certain product IDs appear only in disposal contexts? Or quantities are written off?\n# Also \"warehouse dispatches\" - sometimes disposals are recorded as dispatches to \"waste\" location with negative quantity? Or separate type?\n# Let us look more closely at the full dispatch list for any anomalies.\nimport csv\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nproducts = {}\nwith open('/app/dataset/product_catalog.csv') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    for row in r:\n        pid = row['product_id']\n        products[pid] = row\n\ndispatches = []\nwith open('/app/dataset/warehouse_dispatches.csv') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    for row in r:\n        dispatches.append(row)\n\n# Look for anything odd - duplicate ids? negative qty?\nids = [d['dispatch_id'] for d in dispatches]\nprint('unique ids:', len(set(ids)), 'total:', len(ids))\n# qty\nfor d in dispatches:\n    try:\n        q = int(d['quantity'])\n        if q < 0:\n            print('NEG:', d)\n    except:\n        print('BAD:', d)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
