timestamp=2026-11-04T09:46:17.859Z level=INFO run=92c8de62 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Maybe the spoilage is identified by a specific pattern in the data values themselves?\n# For example, after the temperature excursion, perhaps some products were dispatched at a \"loss\" - but no separate column.\n# Or maybe the dispatches that are disposals have quantity > 0 but the total value is treated differently? \n# Another possibility: look at the full set of dispatches - are there any that are clearly not sales? \n# For example, very high quantity of something weird? Or maybe the Premium Electric Kettle with qty 18 on 2023-07-25 (dispatch_id 169)? That’s a big electronic item - unlikely to be \"spoiled\". \n# Also frozen items after the cold chain break - if frozen goods thawed above safe temp, they might be considered unsafe and disposed. That’s plausible as spoilage.\n# But we need to be \"certain\". The instruction says \"Only exclude a dispatch from revenue if you are certain it represents a disposal event — when in doubt, count it as a sale.\"\n# So only exclude when unmistakable trace exists. What could be such a trace?\n# Maybe a dispatch where the product is recorded as \"spoiled\" version? Or same product but with different product_id? No obvious alt ids.\n# Another idea: check for dispatches happening at odd times (e.g., late night to disposal facility)? Timestamps look like normal business hours.\n# Or maybe there are duplicate dispatches that were voided? No duplicates.\n# Another common pattern: total_weight_kg is zero for disposal? Or very small? Let us look for zero weights.\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/warehouse_dispatches.csv') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    for d in r:\n        if float(d['total_weight_kg']) == 0:\n            print(d)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
