timestamp=2026-11-04T09:46:44.380Z level=INFO run=92c8de62 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# The ground truth score function is given. We need the final answer (integer). \n# Maybe the \"unmistakable trace\" is very subtle? Let us look at the data more. \n# Another possibility: after the freezer temp excursion on 18th, all frozen products dispatched after that event are considered spoiled and written off? Because cold chain was broken - once thawed above safe temp, frozen goods are typically unsellable. That’s a common spoilage scenario.\n# Similarly for meat/dairy that experienced high temps? Meat went up to 15-16C which is well above safe (max ~4C). That’s a serious breach.\n# So all dispatches of products in affected storage categories after the breach might be written off - unless controlled? But we need to be \"certain\". Is it certain that *everything* in those categories after the breach was disposed? Or only specific items?\n# Also \"disposal write-offs are rare\" - if whole categories after one event were written off, that’s a big write-off, not rare. That doesn’t fit.\n# Or maybe only products that were actually in the warehouse during the temperature excursion and then dispatched after? That’s vague. \n# Or look for specific dispatches that are the \"disposal\" events themselves - e.g., dispatching damaged goods to waste? But recorded as normal dispatch with no marker. That’s odd.\n# Another interpretation: the only unmistakable trace might be something like a negative quantity? No negatives. Or a dispatch with same product, same timestamp, but marked as disposal? No. \n# Or maybe the \"spoilage\" is recorded as a dispatch of the same product with quantity listed as damaged - but quantity is positive? Or maybe the weight is suspiciously low relative to quantity (evaporation, thawing loss)? We checked weight vs quantity - all look normal.\n# Another thought: look for dispatches where the product was stored in a zone that exceeded max_safe_temp_c at the time of or shortly before dispatch? That would mean product was exposed to unsafe conditions - but was it disposed or sold? Hard to say. But \"certain\"? Maybe not.\n# Or maybe the disposal is recorded as a separate \"dispatch type\" but not shown? Let us look at all columns again carefully.\nimport csv\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/warehouse_dispatches.csv') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    for row in r:\n        # any other fields hidden? \n        if len(row) != 5:\n            print('len:', len(row), row)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
