timestamp=2026-11-04T09:46:53.393Z level=INFO run=92c8de62 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Let us look at the full dataset differently. Maybe the \"unmistakable trace\" is in the temperature data itself - e.g., a massive spike that indicates complete loss?\n# The temp excursions on 18th: frozen went up briefly to -2.64, meat to 16.26, then recovered quickly. That could cause spoilage, but not necessarily total loss of everything. \n# Another spike? Let us look for other bad readings.\nimport csv\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nbad = []\nwith open('/app/dataset/temperature_logs.csv') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    for row in r:\n        z = row['zone'].strip().lower()\n        t = float(row['temperature_c'].replace(',', '.'))\n        ts = datetime.strptime(row['timestamp'], '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')\n        # check thresholds\n        if z == 'frozen' and t > -10.0:\n            bad.append((ts, z, t))\n        elif z in ('meat', 'dairy') and t > 6.0:\n            bad.append((ts, z, t))\n        elif z == 'dry' and t > 35.0:\n            bad.append((ts, z, t))\nbad.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\nfor b in bad:\n    print(b)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
