timestamp=2026-11-04T09:47:02.513Z level=INFO run=92c8de62 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Only these 6 bad readings total, all on 2023-07-18 within a 6-hour window, then back to normal.\n# That’s a short power failure or cooling system issue? Fixed quickly.\n# Could this have caused some specific spoilage that’s recorded as a \"disposal write-off\" leaving an unmistakable trace?\n# Maybe the disposal is recorded as dispatches of the *affected* products with a specific pattern? \n# For example, immediately after the incident, a large disposal dispatch? Let us look at dispatches right after 18th.\nimport csv\nfrom datetime import datetime\n\nproducts = {}\nwith open('/app/dataset/product_catalog.csv') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    for row in r:\n        pid = row['product_id']\n        ean = row['ean_code']\n        prod = {\n            'name': row['name'],\n            'price': float(row['unit_price'].replace(',', '.')),\n            'cat': row['storage_category'].strip().lower(),\n        }\n        products[pid] = prod\n        products[ean] = prod\n\nwith open('/app/dataset/warehouse_dispatches.csv') as f:\n    r = csv.DictReader(f)\n    for d in r:\n        ts = datetime.strptime(d['timestamp'], '%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S')\n        if ts >= datetime(2023, 7, 18) and ts < datetime(2023, 7, 19):\n            prod = products.get(d['product_id'])\n            if prod and prod['cat'] in ('frozen', 'meat'):\n                print(ts, prod['cat'], prod['name'], d['quantity'], d['product_id'])\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
